  "review": {
    "review": "The hero died after sustained combat pressure. The complete public death receipt identifies a necromancer’s skeleton, resolving the compact report’s unknown attribution. The focused policy defect begins at decision 3044 (log line 8632): at 17/60 HP with invisibility active, the early caster-escape controller selected a zap against an adjacent DM-100 before protected withdrawal could run. The accepted target at decision 3045 (8635) removed invisibility; decision 3047 (8640) emptied the staff and reduced HP to 11. The final retreat at decision 3048 (8643) ended in death. This establishes an arbitration contradiction, not proof that preserving invisibility would guarantee survival. A focused proposed guard makes that early controller yield to existing survival selectors while protection is active below half health. A new reduced public-evidence regression fails on original policy and passes on the candidate; the full suite passes 2,228 tests with four skipped. Independent fresh-state probes reproduce the recorded original attack and select existing retreat on the candidate; they are not historical memory reconstruction or gameplay replay. The adapter derives the changed policy and regression files; files is intentionally empty.",
    "learnings": "Do not let an early caster-escape controller cancel low-health invisibility before protected withdrawal is considered. Preserve existing movement checks and finite retreat budgets. Selecting a different retreat does not establish survival or successful loot recovery."
  },
